## Lore

Robot pilots specialise in the giant humanoid war machines of the Republic of Japan, and they also learn to drive a range of other military vehicles. They are the Republic''s standard pilots: not quite the elite that the SAMAS Samurai and the Glitter Force Troopers are, but highly trained professionals with a broad set of skills.

The public adores them. Japan has long been fascinated by giant war machines, and to ordinary people the robot pilots are manga heroes come to life; television shows, novels and comics about their exploits (and those of the Glitter Boys and SAMAS Samurai) are hugely popular. That glory breeds resentment among the infantry, who feel that they take the real risks without inches of M.D.C. armor around them. Bar brawls on leave have become common enough that there are now separate bars for foot soldiers and for robot and power armor pilots, and one side only visits the other''s when it wants a fight.

## Issued Machines

Power armor and military vehicles are issued for field use only and remain military property. The book names no single machine for this class. The Republic''s robot vehicles are described on printed 158-180; the Armed Forces field ArmaTech designs such as the AT-1053 Ka-Kuma "Metal Bear" heavy artillery support robot and the AT-1063 Hi-Tora "Fire Tiger" light infantry support robot, and have bought Ichto machines such as the IR-2020 Wrecker for military as well as civilian work. The full statistics will be linked here once the book''s robots are in the catalog.

## GM Notes

Cybernetics: 50% of robot pilots start with 1D4 cybernetic implants and may buy more later at their own expense.

The power armor chapter (printed 132) notes that roughly 88% of the Republic''s power armor and robot pilots carry two to six implants, including a head-jack linking them to their machine''s computer and targeting systems, worth +2 on initiative, +1 to roll with impact, +1 to pull punch and +5% to piloting skills. Those bonuses are not stored on this class; apply them only to a pilot who has the head-jack.
', 'published', 'data-script'
